WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:02.480
The Russo Brothers have struck again.

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:06.840
The filmmaking pair have previously admitted
to including scenes in the trailers of their

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:11.490
Marvel films that are misleading or downright
absent from the finished product, and it turns

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.780
out Avengers: Endgame was no different.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.740
Let's break the deception down.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.160
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.780
First, consider this shot of Thor glancing
meaningfully at Stormbreaker.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.520
This is quite obviously a massive red herring;
not only did the shot appear nowhere in the

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.160
film, but Thor looks nothing like this for
the majority of Endgame.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.200
After the film's five-year time jump early
on, we see that the God of Thunder has been

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.740
drowning his sorrows in the recently-established
New Asgard.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:42.460
The trailer's fakeout is almost certainly
a take from the Infinity War shoot, most likely

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:46.510
meant to make us believe that the Avengers
would have to revisit the Battle of Wakanda

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.420
and give Thor one more shot at going for the
head.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.460
"You should've gone for the head."

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.620
Speaking of that time jump, some of the more
misleading moments from the trailers seem

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.160
to be designed to throw us off the scent of
that particular plot point.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.840
For example, Black Widow is seen with her
Infinity War-style blonde locks when Scott

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.710
Lang arrives at the Avengers compound in the
trailer — but in the movie, this takes place

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:15.040
after the jump, and Natasha's hair has mostly
gone back to its natural red shade.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.440
The shot of Natasha getting down with a little
target practice was omitted from the film

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.990
altogether, as was a shot of her sitting outside
in the rain in Japan while on the trail of

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:24.740
Clint Barton.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.520
Another seemingly random alteration: the exchange
between Tony Stark and Steve Rogers before

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.580
the pair jump back to 1970 to retrieve the
Tesseract and more Pym Particles.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.680
While the dialogue exchange in the trailer
is the same as the scene in the film, the

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.500
handshake appears to have simply been added
to promo spots for dramatic effect and to

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.460
let us know that, yes, the two Avengers have
finally mended fences.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:53.840
A more significant difference here is Cap's
uniform; while in the trailers it's the updated

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.660
version from Infinity War, in the movie it's
his Avengers-era original outfit.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:03.280
It's pretty easy to see why this was done
— Cap was sporting his old duds in order

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.680
to blend in during the time-traveling excursion
to the Battle of New York.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:12.060
A few other shots seemingly ended up on the
cutting room floor for no reason in particular,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.840
except perhaps to shave a few minutes off
Endgame's mammoth run time.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.180
These include Bruce Banner agonizing over
the display showing all of the "missing and

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:25.160
presumed dusted" heroes, Clint and Natasha
sharing a private moment, and a poignant shot

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:30.040
of Tony Stark's face as he uses his trashed
Iron Man helmet to record a message to Pepper

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.060
Potts while adrift in space.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:37.540
One other curious exclusion: Captain Marvel's
helmet, which she was seen wearing in the

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.400
trailer, but not during her triumphant return
during Endgame's climactic battle.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.760
There's only one reason we can think of for
this: to hide Carol Danvers' new haircut,

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.540
which she sports after the time jump.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:53.340
Finally, there's another, much more significant
change having to do with another Captain:

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:57.950
Steve Rogers, who is seen in both the trailer
and the film cinching up the straps on his

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:02.500
shield, a look of grim determination on his
face, as all hell appears to be breaking loose

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.260
around him.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:08.460
The spot's version of the sequence is identical
to the one in the movie, save for one crucial

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.460
detail: Cap's shield is whole and unbroken.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:16.560
In the film, it's been busted nearly in half
after a severe pummeling by Thanos, making

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:21.780
Rogers' action all the more badass as he prepares
to charge the Mad Titan with his trusty weapon

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.360
shattered beyond repair.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:28.640
You've gotta hand it to the Russos: they're
dead serious about delivering exciting trailers

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:33.200
while preserving some of their biggest secrets,
as we saw when the trailers for Infinity War

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.700
led us to believe that a fully Hulked-out
Banner would be present at the Battle of Wakanda,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:43.120
when he in fact was forced to suit up in Stark's
Hulkbuster armor due to the not-so-jolly Green

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.280
Giant refusing to come out to play.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.620
Indeed, we're going to go ahead and say that
their trailer trickery is appreciated, as

00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:54.959
Endgame's spots held back details that we
frankly wouldn't have wanted spoiled for us.

00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:58.349
When you're fielding one of the biggest event
films in history, sometimes you've got to

00:03:58.349 --> 00:04:02.860
give the audience what they don't know they
want, like a heaping helping of deception.

